s in which we  can actually find agreement  there's no point in negotiations  proceeding as long  as the eu sticks with this position such  talks would be meaningless and would  take us no nearer to finding a workable  solution  and mr speaker that's the situation we  now face that's why the prime minister  had to make it clear on october the 16th  that the eu had refused to negotiate  seriously for much of the last month or  so  the eu had now at the european council  explicitly ruled out a free trade  agreement with us  like the one that it has with canada and  therefore this country should get ready  for the first of january 2021 with  arrangements that are more like  australia's  based on simple principles of global  free trade  now if the eu wants to change this  situation and i definitely hope it will  it needs to make a fundamental change in  its approach and make clear it's done so  it has to be serious about talking  intensively on all issues  and trying to reach a conclusion and i  hope it will  but it also needs to accept that it's  dealing with an independent and  sovereign country now  we've tried to be clear from the start  that we would not be able to reach an  agreement inconsistent with that status  and i do not think mr speaker that if  you could accuse us  of keeping that a secret and yet the  proposals that the eu discussed with us  in recent weeks which it presents as  compromises  are simply not consistent with our new  sovereign status certainly not yet  so mr speaker well i do not doubt that  many on the eu side are well intentioned  we can't accept the negotiators  proposals require us  to provide full permanent access to our  fishing waters  with quotas substantially unchanged to  those which were imposed  by eu membership we can't operate a  state aid system which is essentially  the same as the eu's  with great discretion given to the eu to  retaliate against us  if it thought that we were deviating  from it and more broadly  we can't accept an arrangement which  means that we stay in step with laws  which have been proposed and adopted by  the eu  across areas of critical national  importance  so in a nutshell mr speaker we've been  asking for no more than what's been  offered in trade agreements to  other global trading countries such as  canada terms which of course the eu said  last year it had no difficulty offering  to us  we're not even asking for special favors  reflecting our 45 years as a member  state  during which we paid in every every day  more than we took out  quite the reverse but even if this  new arrangement is impossible for the eu  that i must inform the house  that we will be leaving on december the  31st  on australian style terms and trading on  the basis of wto rules  now with just 10 weeks left until the  end of the transition period  i have to emphasize that's not my  preferred outcome nor is it the prime  ministers  we recognize that there will be some  turbulence but we've not  come so far to falter now when we are so  close to reclaiming our sovereignty  we have to be in control of our own  borders our fishing grounds we have to  set our own laws  we have to be free to thrive as an  independent free trading nation  embracing the freedoms that flow as a  result  so it's important mr speaker that i turn  to the preparations that we're now  intensifying  for the end of the transition period and  these apply whether we have a free trade  agreement or otherwise of course  now i'm not blind or blase about the  challenges ahead  particularly given the additional  problems that we have dealing with the  covered 19 pandemic  but leaving the eu on australian terms  is an outcome for which we're  increasingly well prepared  ever since the uk decided that it would  leave the single market in the customers  union  on december the 31st government and  businesses alike have been working hard  to prepare  for the new procedures that were the  inevitable result  and i'd like to congratulate businesses  for the resourcefulness they've shown so  far  and we want to work with them so that  they continue responding as  energetically flexibly  and imaginatively as possible to the  challenges of change and we also want to  work with them to prepare for the  opportunities ahead  including those stemming from our new  free trade deals  such as the agreement with japan struck  by my right honourable friend the  secretary of state for international  trade  which of course grants us far more  favorable access to the world's  third biggest economy than we had as an  eu member  and i'd also like to put and recommend  particular thanks to the road haulage  industry  customs intermediaries and others for  their constructive engagement with  government  including at our extensive roundtable  last week  now this week the prime minister and i  will be speaking again to business  leaders  to discuss preparations for life outside  the eu  we'll continue to listen to their  concerns and we will redouble our  efforts to help them to adjust and  prosper  and of course the exo cabinet committee  which meets daily  will intensify its operational focus on  business readiness  we continue to work closely with our  partners in the devolved administrations  because we want to ensure that every  part of the uk is ready for the end  of the transition period and in these  final 10 weeks we're also intensifying  our public information campaign every  firm will find information needs  on new rules which govern trade between  britain and the eu  on gov dot uk forward slash transition  and today hmrc is writing to 200 000  traders that do business with the eu  to reinforce their understanding of the  new customs and tax rules  we're also putting in place i.t systems  to help goods flow across borders  we're giving business access to customs  professionals  to help with new ways of working and  we've also planned how to fast  track vital goods in the first few weeks  to get around eu bureaucracies  we've already published and indeed  updated our border operating model  we've announced 705 million pounds worth  of investment  in jobs infrastructure and technology at  the border  and we've also strengthened our maritime  security  to protect our fishing fleets and  safeguard our seas  and in addition mr speaker to the steps  that we're taking we're also continuing  our work with the eu in the withdrawal  agreement joint committee  and i'd like to update the house on its  latest meeting which took place earlier  this morning  coming only three weeks after the last  meeting i'm pleased to report that in  this  form the approach from the eu is very  constructive  there's a clear imperative on both sides  to find solutions and we remain  committed to working  collaboratively with the eu through the  joint committee process  at our last meeting in brussels i agreed  with my co-chair vice president  shefkovic  that we would intensify discussions to  implement the withdrawal agreement  primarily around citizens rights and the  ni protocol  our officials have since held numerous  sessions and today in london  i reiterated the uk's commitment to  upholding all our obligations under both  the withdrawal agreement  and the belfast agreement we agreed that  we'll publish a joint update on citizens  rights  and i'm pleased to confirm that almost 4  million eu citizens in the uk  have now received status under our  scheme and we also discussed our work  to implement the northern ireland  protocol we're taking steps to implement  new agri-food arrangements  and we also acknowledge the eu's  concerns about appropriate  monitoring of implementation we now have  a better understanding of their requests  and the reasoning behind them  and we've confirmed that the specialized  committee will work intensively in order  to ensure  that we can make progress in this area  and also with respect to gibraltar  and the sovereign base issues now a lot  remains to be resolved before the end of  december  but we have made substantial progress on  implementation  and i look forward to further engagement  with vice president chefkovich in the  weeks ahead  and i want to put on record my personal  appreciation for the constructive tone  and the pragmatic spirit  which with which he and his team have  approached our discussions  mr speaker in his statement on friday  the prime minister looked ahead to 2021  as a year of recovery and renewal when  this government will be focused on  tackling covert 19  and building back better we're getting  ready to do now what the british people  asked of us to forge our own path and  not to acquiesce to anyone else's  agenda and on the negotiations our door  is not closed  it remains a jar and i very much hope  the eu will fundamentally change its  position  but come what may on december the 31st  we will take  back control and so mr speaker i commend  this statement to the house welcome to  shutter minister rachel  thank you mr speaker and can i thank the  minister for advanced site of his  statement this afternoon  at the last general election the prime  minister said that he had an oven ready  deal  ready to go the withdrawal agreement was  a starter course but we're still waiting  for the main meal  a trade agreement with the european  union  at the weekend the right honourable  gentleman was reminded of some of  his previous remarks in 2016  when he assured the country the one  thing that will not change is our  ability to trade freely  with europe even at this late stage  mr speaker labour expects the government  to reach an agreement with the european  union  that honors that commitment it is a  question of competence  and it is a question of trust it is what  this government promised the british  people  of course we were supposed to have a  deal by now this is the third  deadline that the prime minister has set  himself and it's the third  deadline that the prime minister has  missed initially he 