 give up on british industry  and when will the minister be meeting  with those businesses  because it's all well and good to say  prepare but if you can't even bother to  get  a table with them i ask again what are  they supposed to be preparing for and  what support is the government giving  them  now the minister in his statement today  thanked the road haulage industry for  their efforts  you'll also remember mr speaker at  cabinet office questions the  minister said that the road haulage  industry had been far from constructive  so  i welcome the uh thanks he gave them  today but earlier this month  i was with a haulage firm with my right  honourable friend the member for hull  west and hessel  in the hull it's fanta it was a  fantastic business  established over 100 years ago and yet  they don't know  how many of their fleet of lorries will  be able to operate in the eu  come the first of january how mr speaker  and how minister  are they supposed to prepare for that  now government ministers have blindly  referred to leaving with no  trade deal as trading on australian  terms this morning  the business secretary was pressed on  the difference between an australian  deal  and leaving with no deal and he admitted  that it was semantics  semantics mr speaker now they can call  it no deal  they can call it an australia deal they  can call it a narnia deal as far as i'm  concerned  but let's be honest about what that  means and let's be honest about how  damaging it is  for this country now the prime minister  promised  and someone says from a sedentary place  it's not damaging 10  tariffs on british cars being exported  to the european union  that is damage 40 tariffs on land being  exported to the european union  that is damaged and if any member wants  to stand up and tell their constituents  tell british industry and tell british  farming that that is not damaging  be my guess but it is not the truth now  the prime minister promised the uk would  prosper mightily without a trade deal  with the eu  so given that confidence will the  government publish its full economic  impact assessment  on the implications if no trade deal is  achieved broken down by industry and  broken down  by the regions and nations of our united  kingdom  it may at least help us to understand  what we're supposed to be planning for  and while the government lectures  businesses about being match fit  the government's own preparations are  badly off-pace  so let's look at those in late july the  government announced 50 million pounds  of funding for customs intermediaries  could the minister update the house with  how much of this the funds  businesses have drawn down and the  latest figures for the number of customs  agents  trained up to be ready for the 1st of  january  the government have given authorisation  for a number of lorry parks so how many  of these  inland border facilities as the minister  likes to call them has been started work  on  and how many are now completed of the  i.t systems needed  can the minister list how many have been  um how many  um are on track and whether the crucial  goods vehicle movement system  has been tested with all haulage  businesses  the minister mentions business  preparedness so let me just touch  on that what is happening in terms of  security  and what is happening in terms of data  sharing i'll just conclude  mr speaker this government must deliver  a deal which provides guarantees and  safeguards on workers rights  environmental standards  and animal welfare protect jobs and do  nothing to jeopardize the good friday  agreement this was all promised last  year  time is short so my message to  government is blunt stop posturing  start negotiating and prop deliver the  deal that you promised to the british  people  [Applause]  i'm very grateful to the honourable lady  for her questions and she  she made the point that uh uh with  respect to an oven ready deal that the  withdrawal agreement  of course was uh concluded happily and  voted on by this house of commons and so  we had a deal a deal which many on those  benches opposed but a deal which now  means  that our destiny is certain that we are  fulfilling the wishes of the british  people in the same way as 52 percent of  the population of the united kingdom and  53 percent  of the population of leeds west her  inconsistency invaded to leave  we will leave we were honoring  honoring our commitment to the british  people she also  was kind enough to refer to some of the  past statements that i made which were  quoted on the television briefly  at the weekend i have to say and this is  no reflection  on her but if she's going to talk about  past  statements she'd better clear that with  the leader of the opposition who in the  past  has simultaneously favored eu membership  then said he would accept the referendum  where we left then said that we needed a  second referendum in order to satisfy  the first then so we should have an  extension on our membership  of the european union in the transition  period and now is silent on all  of those questions  it's a statement on the government  policy not the policy of the opposition  yes i was merely pointing out mr speaker  that we had an oven ready deal  and on the labor side we had  we had an indigestible dog's breakfast  and a leader of the opposition who will  not  eat his words um the um  the honorable lady also asked about uh  the the various deadlines those  deadlines are deadlines that the uk  government has set but the eu have not  met  in any negotiation both sides have to  honor their commitments and as i pointed  out in my statement and the honourable  lady did not  uh uh of course acknowledge this  we were available to talk uh every day  in the weeks preceding the european  council  and the european union were not but  but i have to say that our firmness on  this proposition i believe is now  bearing fruit  even as we were exchanging thoughts  across the di